Ruby PHP Serializer
===================

This module provides two methods: PHP.serialize() and PHP.unserialize(), both
of which should be compatible with the similarly named functions in PHP.

Basic usage:

 require 'php_serialize'
 in = {'foo' => 'bar'}
 php = PHP.serialize(in)
 # pass string to PHP unserialize() to get array('foo' => 'bar')
 out = PHP.unserialize(php) # => {'foo' => 'bar'}


PHP.unserialize can also read PHP sessions, which are collections of named
serialized objects.  These can be reserialized using PHP.serialize_session(),
which has the same semantics as PHP.serialize(), but which only supports
Hash and associative Arrays for the root object.


Untrusted input
===============

A PHP 'O:' record names the class to build and the attributes to assign.
Before 1.2.0, PHP.unserialize() honoured that name: it looked the class up in
the constant namespace, called .new on it and then called {attr}= for every
attribute in the payload.  A value taken from a database column, a cookie or a
request parameter could therefore choose which class in your process got
instantiated and which setters ran.  Those side effects happen during
unserialize(), so rescuing the return value does not undo them.

Since 1.2.0 an object record is only built if the caller allows that class:

 # Data only -- any 'O:' record raises PHP::UnserializeError.
 # Use this for anything you did not serialize yourself.
 PHP.unserialize_data(untrusted)

 # Allow specific classes, by their capitalized PHP name.
 PHP.unserialize(str, {:Teststruct => TestStruct})

 # Attributes as a plain Hash, instantiating nothing.
 PHP.unserialize(str, :on_object => :data)

 # Pre-1.2.0 constant lookup.  Only for input you produced yourself.
 PHP.unserialize(str, :on_object => :constants)

Nesting is bounded at PHP::MAX_DEPTH (512 by default, :max_depth per call),
because SystemStackError would escape the caller's rescue.

The default is the strict mode: an unknown class raises
PHP::UnserializeError (a TypeError subclass).  Attribute names must be plain
identifiers and must resolve to a public writer on the target, so PHP's
mangled private property names ("\0Class\0prop") are rejected rather than
sent.  Malformed or truncated input raises PHP::UnserializeError too, instead
of silently returning a partial value.


